WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:08.180
“I've already grown a goiter from this torture,
My stomach's squashed under my chin, my beard's

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.070
pointing at heaven, my brain's crushed in
a casket,

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.969
my breast twists like a harpy's.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:18.260
My brush,
above me all the time, dribbles paint

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:26.280
so my face makes a fine floor for droppings!
my poor ass strains to work as a counterweight,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.230
every gesture I make is blind and aimless.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:36.500
My skin hangs loose below me, my spine's
all knotted from folding over itself.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.850
My painting is dead.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:45.100
I am not in the right place—I am not a painter.”

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:51.350
Would it surprise you if I told you that these
lines were scribed by none other than Michelangelo,

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.940
painter of the Sistine Chapel?

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:59.880
We often gaze upon masterpieces so miraculous
as this and imagine it as a piece born from

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:06.530
a desire to create great art, with passion
pouring from the artist’s every pore.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:14.130
Michelangelo in fact, hated his grand epitome
of human creation, finding it quite the bore.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.400
Yet over five million people flock to admire
it every year.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.900
Michelangelo loathed every minute of its creation,
so much so that he wrote the preceding poem.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.290
So then why did he do it?

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.970
Well, he was being paid by the church.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:34.400
And as we all know, enough money will make
a man sell his soul, or paint a giant fresco

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.750
on the Pope’s ceiling, either are just as
likely.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:43.730
‘A picture paints a thousand words’ this
well-known idiom reveals the most important

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:50.570
aspect of artwork, that it is potentially
the most powerful medium we have to record

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.180
and be able to reflect on history in a visual
way.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:02.430
Today every relevant and far more often irrelevant
moment of human creation is captured in photography,

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.820
frequently archived publically for the world
to view.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:12.019
When Apollo 11 landed the first two men on
the moon in 1969 it was captured fantastically

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:18.150
on film and broadcast for the world to see.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:23.349
We can all observe in precise detail what
happened in that momentous moment.

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:28.939
But until the advent of photography, we had
to rely on two mediums to guide us through

00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:36.010
the triumphs and tribulations of times gone
by, writing and illustration.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:39.609
When Leonardo Da Vinci invented his flying
machine he probably would have really liked

00:02:39.609 --> 00:02:45.989
to make an obnoxiously gloat-filled Instagram
post, but he was thankfully resigned to drawing

00:02:45.989 --> 00:02:50.780
an impressively detailed yet beautifully artistic
sketch instead.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:57.049
Needless to say, writing is a powerful force
but only art can influence a nation with a

00:02:57.049 --> 00:02:58.930
single glance.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.739
Artwork drags us from the drudgery of our
pixel-perfect period in time and grants us

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:09.300
a wonderfully dynamic perspective on the events
that changed our world.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:16.180
Artwork allows us to instantly visualise moments
such as the signing of the Declaration of

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:21.689
Independence, the invention of Gutenberg's
printing press, the French revolution, the

00:03:21.689 --> 00:03:28.330
protestant reformation, the fall of the Roman
Empire and that elusive period during the

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:32.889
middle ages when the world was full of murderous
giant rabbits.

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:39.620
And alike these freakishly satanic rabbits,
famous pieces of artwork aren’t always historically

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:45.969
accurate, George Washington certainly didn’t
cross the Delaware like this.

00:03:45.969 --> 00:03:51.450
And Napoleon definitely didn’t look as fabulous
as this whilst crossing the Alps.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:57.829
For a start, he would have ridden a mule,
not a horse, as Paul Delaroche depicted in

00:03:57.829 --> 00:04:02.540
this far more realistic envisioning of the
event, painted in 1850.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.379
But, you know what?

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:07.689
These inaccuracies don’t matter.

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:13.629
The purpose of great art is not to provide
an exact recount of the event in question,

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:16.549
we have historical textbooks for that.

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:22.139
Instead, art provides a stylised, sometimes
idealised version of events that aids our

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:27.940
imagination and with one glance transports
us to that precious moment in time.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:34.410
It is only through this spectacular visualisation
of art that allows it to perforate popular

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:38.970
culture and bring history to the masses.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:44.099
It is not for us to imagine the ambience of
a cafe terrace in Southern France in the late

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:50.710
evening of 1888, on this, Van Gogh has us
covered.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:56.800
Also in the late 19th century, one artist’s
paintings helped all of Europe visualise a

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:06.110
new world and caused mass migration, thus
by no exaggeration, one man’s artwork built

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:07.919
the American West.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:14.150
More specifically, German-American painter
Albert Bierstadt’s rich vistas of the living

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:21.240
landscapes of the American West were brought
back to London in 1867 and exhibited to the

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:27.710
masses, including a private exhibition for
Queen Victoria.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:34.090
So mesmerised by the idyllic freedom depicted
upon these canvases, so beautifully promised

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:40.830
was the vast bounty of the free new world
laid bare to all, that British, German and

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:46.900
Irish people packed up their lives and migrated
to America in their millions.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:53.930
It has been said that European migration to
the states was fueled by Bierstadt’s art.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:06:01.210
Art can and has been used to mobilise entire
nations into mass hysteria, into war, into

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:06.669
peace or to simply mobilise them, to colonise
new lands.

00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:14.800
Art doesn’t just change what we do, it changes
how we think and what we think changes the

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.810
type of images we prefer to look at.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.970
Psychological studies have demonstrated how
our personalities are reflected in the types

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:29.300
of artwork we find pleasing to look at and
thus what we choose to hang on our walls.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:36.000
There is a very real and predictable positive
correlation between conservatives and highly

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.940
representational artwork.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:45.250
In other words, people who have a more conservative
outlook on life prefer traditional artwork,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.990
artwork that looks like what it seeks to represent.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:54.930
For instance, when 19th-century Belgian Eugène
Joors set out to paint a man playing a guitar

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:01.949
he, by some miracle of humanity, ended up
painting a man with a guitar.

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:08.660
Meanwhile, at the same time in France, cubist
Georges Braque attempted the same feat, to

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:14.629
paint a man with a guitar, yet somehow painted
a tear in the spacetime fabric.

00:07:14.629 --> 00:07:22.460
Conversely, liberals considerably prefer abstract
art forms such as cubism, surrealism and modern

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.460
art.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:28.240
Such as Banett Newman’s Onement VI, alternatively
known as ‘blue square with line down the

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.639
middle’, which recently sold for $43.8 million.

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:41.889
But this is not nearly as ridiculous as the
‘artwork’ not on display at the Museum

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:43.830
of Non-Visible Art.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:49.789
You heard that correctly, the Museum of Non-Visible
Art is exactly that, a gallery of empty rooms

00:07:49.789 --> 00:07:51.430
and empty walls.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:56.800
Each blank wall is accompanied by a title
and description card that describes the artwork

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.550
you are supposedly ‘looking at’.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:04.849
It is up to your mind to imagine what the
art should look like.

00:08:04.849 --> 00:08:09.180
I’m sorry but if I p aid to see the latest
blockbuster at the cinema and I was told I

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:14.389
have to imagine what the film looks like for
2 hours because it’s a ‘non-visible film’,

00:08:14.389 --> 00:08:18.159
it’s reasonable to say I would be beyond
pissed.

00:08:18.159 --> 00:08:27.259
Yet, most incredible of all, this gallery
made a sale, in 2011 a woman paid $10,000

00:08:27.259 --> 00:08:30.230
for the piece titled ‘Fresh Air’.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:36.770
In return for her ten fat Gs she received
a card with a description of the artwork,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:41.150
which she could presumably stick to an empty
wall at home and have to describe to all her

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:46.620
family and friends why there is a gaping white
space in her living room and an idiotically

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.940
larger space in her wallet.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:55.820
An art magazine described the piece like so
‘A unique piece, only this one is for sale.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.240
The air you are purchasing is like buying
an endless tank of oxygen.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.440
No matter where you are, you always have the
ability to take a breath of the most delicious,

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.050
clean-smelling air that the earth can produce.

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:14.079
Every breath you take gives you endless peace
and health.

00:09:14.079 --> 00:09:18.230
This artwork is something to carry with you
if you own it.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:22.910
Because wherever you are, you can imagine
yourself getting the most beautiful taste

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:32.389
of air that is from the mountain tops or fields
or from the ocean side; it is an endless supply.’

00:09:32.389 --> 00:09:39.390
But how does art affect us in our modern day-to-day
lives?

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:44.730
We see artwork everywhere we go, sometimes
in our homes, doctor’s waiting rooms, in

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:45.730
the workplace.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:51.460
Whether it be a print of a beautiful renaissance
fresco by Raphael looming luxuriously behind

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:57.709
a receptionist’s head or one of those bizarre
iron irrationalities of human form placed

00:09:57.709 --> 00:10:02.451
unaccountably on your mother’s coffee table
because she thinks it ‘adds a touch of class

00:10:02.451 --> 00:10:04.209
to the room’.

00:10:04.209 --> 00:10:09.550
Even if we pay little conscious attention
to any of these pieces positioned throughout

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:16.470
our lives, they actually do affect us considerably
and the very presence of art changes the way

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.300
we think and behave.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:24.000
Recent research by Exeter University’s School
of Psychology concluded that employees who

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:30.779
work in an office decorated with art are 17%
more productive than those who work in a blank

00:10:30.779 --> 00:10:31.889
office space.

00:10:31.889 --> 00:10:36.370
Other research conducted by the University
of Westminster found that viewing art for

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:44.070
a small amount of time each day reduces cortisol
(stress levels), increases our empathy towards

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:51.149
others, releases dopamine, increases our critical
thinking abilities by up to 18% and it can

00:10:51.149 --> 00:10:56.779
also help to reduce mental exhaustion in the
exact same way that spending time outdoors

00:10:56.779 --> 00:10:57.779
does.

00:10:57.779 --> 00:11:02.889
So there you go, instead of going outside,
hang a picture of a field on your wall.

00:11:02.889 --> 00:11:09.930
A study in 2014 also found that creating or
viewing art increases long-term physical resilience

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.870
and can actually delay ageing.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:17.389
Art is inextricably tangled with our humanity.

00:11:17.389 --> 00:11:22.880
We don’t only appreciate it when it’s
done well, we have an innate need, a desire

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.160
to create and look at it.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:30.209
When our ancient hunter-gatherer ancestors
crafted tools needed to survive, they took

00:11:30.209 --> 00:11:33.480
extra time to make them symmetrical and attractive.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:39.970
For no other reason than it gave them satisfaction
to use ascetically pleasing tools, rather

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:45.019
than ugly ones, also because they had bugger
all else to do.

00:11:45.019 --> 00:11:51.459
Throughout modernity, in building our houses
and public spaces, all civilisations the world

00:11:51.459 --> 00:11:58.029
over have spent huge amounts of extra money,
time and manpower to make them visually appealing.

00:11:58.029 --> 00:12:04.740
In a YouGov poll, 77% of respondents said
they prefer traditional architecture, with

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:12.269
its ornate beauty over cold contemporary buildings,
with their hostile steel and glass facades.

00:12:12.269 --> 00:12:18.519
The data shows that across the UK buildings
with traditional architecture and even neo-traditional

00:12:18.519 --> 00:12:23.440
architecture sell for 15 per cent more than
modern housing.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:28.649
London houses built before the first world
war, you know when new builds were actually

00:12:28.649 --> 00:12:35.290
attractive still, went up in value 465 per
cent over the past thirty years.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:42.430
Whereas their newer, post-war counterparts,
well their value rose by only 255 per cent

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:44.850
in the same period.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:50.700
People pay a huge premium for beauty and good
architecture is art.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:57.209
Why do we pay so much more, for a few details
in our brickwork and around our window frames?

00:12:57.209 --> 00:13:02.960
Because humans are hard-wired to seek out
and derive pleasure from beauty.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:09.240
The research on this is clear and substantive,
living in a beautiful area, amongst beautiful

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:15.810
houses, which one could equate to living in
a piece of artwork, makes us happier, less

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.460
stressed and healthier.

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:23.880
And bringing that beauty into your home, whether
its a painting that brings you joy or a sculpture

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:28.360
you admire, will only increase these effects.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:34.000
So why don’t you seek to bring happiness
to others and change the world by creating

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.079
your own art to be admired by society.

00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:43.740
